  "name": "Question 6",
  "problem": "If the line $y=-2x+3$ and the curve $y=\\frac{2}{x-2}+1$ intersect at the point $(p,q),$ then $p+q=$",
  "worked_solution": "We need to solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=-2x+3 &  \\\\ y=\\frac{2}{x-2}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and solving, we get a quadratic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2x+3 & =\\frac{2}{x-2}+1 \\\\ -2x+2 & =\\frac{2}{x-2} \\\\ (-2x+2)(x-2) & =2 \\\\ 2(-x+1)(x-2) & =2 \\\\ (-x+1)(x-2) & =1 \\\\ -{x}^{2}+3x-2 & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}-3x+3 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the quadratic formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{-3\\pm \\sqrt{{3}^{2}-4(1)(3)}}{2(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3\\pm \\sqrt{9-12}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3\\pm \\sqrt{-3}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe square root of a negative number is not a real number, so there are no real solutions. Consequently, the line $y=-2x+3$ does not intersect the hyperbola $y=\\frac{2}{x-2}+1.$\n\nA sketch of the situation is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066764/assets/41fee04552a47d93f1dbd788e69e12c4b58e377ec4c0b63c3ed55c712d337f94.png)",
